## Solution

We find all differentiable $f:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ satisfying

$$f(x+y+z)+3xyz=f(x)+f(y)+f(z)+3(x+y+z)(xy+yz+zx)-2. \tag{1}$$

**Step 1: Rewrite using the cube identity.** Recall the identity

$$(x+y+z)^3 = x^3+y^3+z^3+3(x+y+z)(xy+yz+zx)-3xyz,$$

which follows from expanding $(x+y+z)(xy+yz+zx)=x^2y+x^2z+xy^2+y^2z+xz^2+yz^2+3xyz$. Hence

$$3(x+y+z)(xy+yz+zx)-3xyz=(x+y+z)^3-x^3-y^3-z^3.$$

Rearranging (1),

$$f(x+y+z)=f(x)+f(y)+f(z)+3(x+y+z)(xy+yz+zx)-3xyz-2,$$

so

$$f(x+y+z)=f(x)+f(y)+f(z)+(x+y+z)^3-x^3-y^3-z^3-2. \tag{2}$$

**Step 2: Reduce to Cauchy.** Define $g(x)=f(x)-x^3$. Substituting into (2) and cancelling $(x+y+z)^3$ against $x^3+y^3+z^3$:

$$g(x+y+z)=g(x)+g(y)+g(z)-2. \tag{3}$$

Now set $h(x)=g(x)-1$, i.e. $h(x)=f(x)-x^3-1$. Then (3) becomes

$$h(x+y+z)-1=(h(x)-1)+(h(y)-1)+(h(z)-1)-2=h(x)+h(y)+h(z)-5,$$

so

$$h(x+y+z)=h(x)+h(y)+h(z). \tag{4}$$

Since $f$ is differentiable, so is $h$.

**Step 3: Solve the reduced equation.** Setting $y=z=0$ in (4) gives $h(x)=h(x)+2h(0)$, hence $h(0)=0$. Setting $z=0$ gives

$$h(x+y)=h(x)+h(y),$$

the Cauchy functional equation. A differentiable solution of Cauchy's equation is linear: $h(x)=ax$ for some constant $a\in\mathbb{R}$ (differentiability at $0$ gives $h'(0)=a$, and $h(x)=xh(1)$ for rational $x$, then continuity extends to all reals).

**Step 4: Recover $f$ and verify.** From $h(x)=ax$,

$$f(x)=x^3+ax+1,\qquad a\in\mathbb{R}.$$

Verification: with $f(t)=t^3+at+1$,

- LHS of (1): $(x+y+z)^3+a(x+y+z)+1+3xyz$.
- RHS of (1): $x^3+y^3+z^3+a(x+y+z)+3+3(x+y+z)(xy+yz+zx)-2$ $=x^3+y^3+z^3+a(x+y+z)+1+3(x+y+z)(xy+yz+zx)$.

The difference LHS $-$ RHS equals

$$(x+y+z)^3+3xyz-\big(x^3+y^3+z^3+3(x+y+z)(xy+yz+zx)\big)=0$$

by the cube identity. So every function of this form satisfies (1).

**Conclusion.** The differentiable solutions are exactly

$$\boxed{f(x)=x^3+ax+1,\quad a\in\mathbb{R}.}$$

